  "narrative": "I now have all the evidence needed. The enrichment is highly significant (FDR=0.0), three drivers are confirmed in the pathway, and two biosynthetic steps are verified as adjacent KEGG reactions. Here is the narrative:\n\n---\n\n## Biological Interpretation: Steroid Hormone Metabolism Disruption\n\n**Affected Pathways.** The dominant signal is a near-perfect enrichment for **Androgen and Estrogen Metabolism** (KEGG map00150; SMPDB SMP00068; FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment ≈ 1001). Five of the ten input metabolites — **dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA, C01227)**, **androstenedione (C00280)**, **testosterone (C00535)**, **dihydrotestosterone (DHT, C03917)**, and **estradiol (C00951)** — map directly to this pathway, making it the unambiguous top hit.\n\n**Key Driver Compounds.** DHEA emerges as the most upstream driver in the steroidogenic cascade. The KEGG reaction graph confirms a direct, reversible enzymatic step from DHEA → Androstenedione (1 reaction hop), and a second direct step from Androstenedione → Testosterone (1 reaction hop). Together, DHEA, androstenedione, testosterone, and DHT form a coherent androgen biosynthetic module. The additional presence of estradiol anchors this to the androgen-to-estrogen aromatization branch, implicating altered flux across the entire steroidogenic axis.\n\n**Biological Significance.** Coordinated elevation or differential abundance of these five steroids points to disruption of steroid hormone biosynthesis — a pathway tightly linked to endocrine signalling, reproductive biology, and cancer metabolism. DHEA and its downstream androgens are well-documented drivers of prostate and breast cancer cell proliferation. Estradiol dysregulation adds a parallel estrogenic dimension. The parallel inclusion of thromboxane, dobutamine, quinolinic acid, dehydroascorbic acid, and 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutaric acid suggests the treatment may simultaneously affect platelet function, cardiovascular signalling, neuroinflammation (via quinolinic acid), and oxidative stress — but the steroid axis is the dominant, statistically prioritized event.",
